3.1.1.m:
That which is your lunar mansion, Mṛgaśīrṣa,
dear, O King, dearest of the dear,
to that [mansion] of yours, O Soma, may we pay worship with oblation;
be peace to our two-footed, peace to our four-footed!
Rudra comes expanding with Ārdrā,
the best of the gods, the lord of the unslayable [cows];
may we pay worship to his lunar mansion with oblation;
may he not harm our offspring nor our heroes!
May the missile of Rudra pass around us;
may the Ārdrā lunar mansion enjoy our oblation!

Bṛhaspati, being born first,
came into being over the Tiṣya lunar mansion,
the best of the gods, victorious in battles;
along all quarters may there be fearlessness for us!
May Tiṣya protect us in front and in the middle;
may Bṛhaspati protect us from behind!
May the two ward off enmity, may they make fearlessness;
may we be masters of heroic strength!
May this oblation be pleasing to the serpents,
whose intent the Āśreṣās follow!

Aryaman, the unaging, mighty king,
the bull of the Phalgunīs, roars loudly.
Best of the gods, O Bhaga, you are Bhaga;
the Phalgunīs know that of you; know that!
Push together here for us unaging regal power,
accompanied by heroic strength, rich in cows, rich in horses!
Bhaga indeed is the giver, Bhaga is the distributor;
Bhaga entered the divine Phalgunīs;
may we go to that instigation of Bhaga,
where we may revel in joint revelry with the gods!

3.2.7.m:
He places light in the intermediate space;
he places light as the sun in yonder world.
Luminous become these worlds for him
who knows thus.
"You are steadfast; make firm the earth," he says;
by this he makes firm the earth.
"You are a support; make firm the intermediate space," he says;
by this he makes firm the intermediate space.
"You are a foundation; make firm the sky," he says;
by this he makes firm the sky.

"You are order; make firm the directions," he says;
by this he makes firm the directions.
By these he makes firm these worlds.
These worlds become firm for him with offspring and domestic animals,
who knows thus.
At first he sets down three potsherds;
three are these worlds,
for the attainment of these worlds.
At first he sets down one potsherd;
one potsherd [of the skull], indeed, first comes into being in a man.

that [is commensurate] with the Jagatī.
Setting down the potsherds commensurate with the meters,
he makes firm these worlds in order, and the directions for support.
Then he places lifespan, breaths, offspring, and domestic animals in the sacrificer;
he makes his kinsmen abundant all around him.
"You are piled up," he says;
this is just as the formula [prescribes].
"Be heated by the heat of the Bhṛgus and the Aṅgirases," he says;
he heats them with the heat of the deities.
Then, when it is concluded,
with the four-footed verse, "The potsherds which the wise arrange in the heat," he unlooses [them].
Domestic animals are four-footed;
he firmly establishes [himself] upon domestic animals in the end.

3.2.8.m:
"At the impulse of the divine Savitṛ," he says, for impelling.
"With the arms of the Aśvins," he says;
for the Aśvins were the Adhvaryus of the gods.
"With the hands of Pūṣan," he says, for guiding.
"I pour you together," he says;
according to the deities he pours them together.
"Waters have met with waters, plants with sap," he says.
The waters quicken the plants;
the plants quicken the waters;
they quicken each other.

"You are the caldron, having all life," he says;
he places all life in the sacrificer.
"Spread wide; may the lord of the sacrifice spread wide," he says;
he spreads wide the sacrificer with offspring and domestic animals.
"Take on a skin," he says;
he makes it completely endowed with a body.
Then, having brought water, he smooths it around;
he thereby places skin upon the flesh.
Therefore the flesh is covered by skin.
This caldron, indeed, unappeased,

is placed upon the fire half-month by half-month,
[namely] that which is the sacrificial cake.
It is capable of burning up the sacrificer with heat.
He carries fire around [it];
he makes it an animal,
for appeasement, so that it will not burn up.
Three times he carries fire around [it];
for threefold is the sacrifice,
and also for the driving away of the demons.
"Warded off is the demon, warded off are the enemies," he says,

He covers it with the broom;
therefore the head is covered with hair.
He does not become bald
who knows thus.
The sacrificial cake is the likeness of the sacrificial animal.
It must not be covered without a formula;
it would be [done] at random;
the domestic animals of the sacrificer would be liable to perish.
"Unite with brahman," he says.
The vital breaths, indeed, are brahman.

‘Deposit your bodies in me;
I will generate for you him
on whom you shall wipe [it] off.’
Those gods deposited their bodies in Agni.
Therefore they say:
‘Agni is all the deities.’
With an ember he cast water [into the coals];
thence Ekata was born.
He cast a second [drop];

thence Dvita was born.
He cast a third [drop];
thence Trita was born.
Because they were born from the waters (āpaḥ),
that is the nature of the Āpyas (āpyatva).
Because they were born from the bodies (ātman),
that is the nature of the Ātmyas (ātmyatva).
Those gods wiped off [the pollution] on the Āpyas;
the Āpyas wiped [it] off on one upon whom the sun rises [while sleeping];
he upon whom the sun rises, on one upon whom the sun sets [while sleeping];

he upon whom the sun sets, on a bad-nailed man;
the bad-nailed man, on a dark-toothed man;
the dark-toothed man, on an agradidhiṣu;
the agradidhiṣu, on a parivitta;
the parivitta, on a killer of a hero;
the killer of a hero, on a killer of a Brahmin; that [pollution] did not pass beyond the killer of a Brahmin.
He pours [the wash water] within the sacrificial altar to secure [it].
He holds [the cake] over with a firebrand for cookedness;
for the gods indeed desire cooked food.

3.2.9.m:
‘At the impulse of god Savitṛ,’ he says, [and] takes the wooden sword for instigation.
‘With the arms of the Aśvins,’ he says;
for the Aśvins were the two Adhvaryus of the gods.
‘With the hands of Pūṣan,’ he says for restraint.
‘I take [thee]; thou art Indra’s right arm,’ he says;
he places virile power into the sacrificer.
‘Thousand-spiked, hundred-pointed,’ he says;
he describes this very form [and] greatness of it.
‘Thou art Vāyu, sharp-pointed,’ he says;
Vāyu is fiery energy;

he places fiery energy into this [sacrificer].
There was an asura named Viṣādh.
He was afraid:
‘The gods will overcome me with sacrifice.’
He vomited upon the earth;
she became unhallowed.
Moreover, when Indra slew Vṛtra,
his blood ran across the earth;
she became unhallowed.
‘O earth, place of divine worship,’ he says;

he makes her hallowed, a place of divine worship.
‘May I not harm the root of thine plant,’ he says,
for the non-injury of the plants.
‘Go to the fold, the cow-pen,’ he says;
the meters are the fold, the cow-pen;
he makes the meters a fold, a cow-pen for him.
‘May the sky rain upon thee,’ he says;
the sky is rain;
he secures rain.
‘Bind [him], O god Savitṛ, in the furthest distance,’ he says;

he strikes him away from the intermediate space.
He carries away a third [handful];
he strikes him away from heaven.
He carries away a fourth [handful] silently;
he strikes him away from the unmeasured.
In the beginning this [earth] belonged to the asuras;
as far as a seated man looks out,
so much [alone] belonged to the gods.
Those gods said:
‘Let there be [a share] in this for us as well.’

‘How much will you give us?’ [they asked].
‘As much as you can enclose yourselves,’ [replied the asuras].
They enclosed it from the south, [saying,] ‘May the Vasus [enclose] thee!’;
from behind, [saying,] ‘May the Rudras [enclose] thee!’;
from the north, [saying,] ‘May the Ādityas [enclose] thee!’
With Agni they conquered eastward;
with the Vasus southward;
with the Rudras westward;
with the Ādityas northward.
For him who knows thus, when they enclose the sacrificial altar,

he prospers in himself;
his rival is defeated.
‘At the impulse of the god Savitṛ,’ he says for instigation.
‘The wise perform the work,’ he says;
for a work is performed when impelled.
The hallowed and the unhallowed went apart on the earth:
the eastward and northward is hallowed,
the westward and southward is unhallowed.
He makes [the altar] slanting toward the east and north;
he makes her hallowed, a place of divine worship.

He raises the two shoulders of the sacrificial altar toward the east
for the enclosure of the Āhavanīya fire;
[he makes] the two hips toward the west
for the enclosure of the Gārhapatya fire;
and also for pairing.
He digs [the earth];
whatever of it is unhallowed,
that he strikes away.
He digs; therefore plants perish.

He cuts the root;
he cuts the very root of the rival.
If the root were left standing, the rakṣases would leap up along with it.
If he were to cut it with his hand,
his offspring would be bad-nailed.
He cuts with the wooden sword;
the wooden sword is indeed a vajra;
with the vajra he strikes away the rakṣases from the sacrifice.
If dug too deep, it belongs to the ancestors as deities;
he digs only so much:

measured with Prajāpati, the mouth of the sacrifice.
The sacrificial altar hid from the gods;
they found it at [a depth of] four finger-breadths.
Therefore it should be dug four finger-breadths [deep].
He digs four finger-breadths [deep];
for plants are established at four finger-breadths.
He digs for a firm foundation;
he brings the sacrificer to a firm foundation.
He makes it higher on the south side;
he has made the very form of the place of divine worship.

3.2.10.m:
If he should set them near from east to west,
he would cause the kindling wood to arrive away from the path of the oblations.
The sacrificial grass is offspring;
he would miss the procreation of offspring with the sacrificial grass.
He sets them near from west to east;
he causes the kindling wood to arrive by the path of the oblations;
he meets the procreation of offspring directly with the sacrificial grass.
The kindling wood is to the south,
the sacrificial grass to the north.
The kindling wood is the self;
the sacrificial grass is offspring;
for offspring are farther north of oneself at the crossing place.
Having brought the sacrificial substance near from there,
he establishes it according to each deity.
The sacrificer firmly establishes himself with offspring and domestic animals.

3.3.1.m:
'Burned away is the rakṣas, burned away are the spiteful foes,' he says,
to strike away the rakṣases.
'With the hottest fiery energy of Agni I scorch you,' he says for fitness for sacrifice.
He cleanses the offering ladles:
the dipping spoon first;
he prepares a male for them for the sake of pairing;
then the juhū;
then the upabhṛt;
then the dhruvā.
Yonder [sky] is the juhū;

the intermediate space is the upabhṛt;
the earth is the dhruvā.
These worlds are the offering ladles;
the cleaning grasses are rain.
Rain prepares these worlds in order;
then, prepared, they prosper.
These worlds prosper with offspring and domestic animals for him
who knows thus.
If he should desire that Parjanya should become rainy,
he should cleanse from the tip.

For this [arm] is an offering ladle.
The dipping spoon is exhalation;
the juhū is the right hand;
the upabhṛt is the left;
the dhruvā is the self;
the cleansing grasses are food.
Having become inhalation at the mouth,
food, having entered the self,
beautifies the body from without.
Therefore he cleanses the dipping spoon first;
for having become inhalation at the mouth,
food enters the self.
They are exhalation and inhalation.
He is not deprived of exhalation and inhalation
who knows thus.

3.3.2.m:
'The artwork of the sky stretched down,
resting on the crest of the earth,
with that thousand-branched one
we destroy the rival, svāhā,' [with this formula] he throws the cleaning grasses of the ladles into the fire.
Darba grasses are water;
he thus proclaims their form and their greatness.
With an anuṣṭubh verse [he throws them];
Prajāpati is connected with the anuṣṭubh;
the broom of grass belongs to Prajāpati;
the cleaning grasses of the ladles are the top of the broom.

He equips them with their own meter,
with their own deity.
Moreover, the verse is female;
darbha grass is male;
that is a pair.
He thus makes a pair of it in the sacrifice for procreation.
The sacrificer propagates with offspring and domestic animals.
Some cast them away at random;
that must not be done so.
That would be a draining of the sacrificial act that has been undertaken.
